{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good evening. I’m Dr. Lee, one of the hospitalists. I understand you came in with chest pain. Can you tell me more about what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, thank you. I was watching TV when I felt a tightness in my chest, right in the middle, and it went into my left shoulder. I’ve had this before, similar to my last heart episode."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:17:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Did it go away on its own? Did you try nitroglycerin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:18:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I took three doses, but it didn’t help much. It only eased when I got to the ER and they gave me morphine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. You’ve had multiple heart events before, correct? And a kidney transplant?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:19:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I had the transplant years ago. And I’ve had a few non-ST heart attacks. My cardiologist has been managing it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:20:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve reviewed your records. Your troponin level is normal, and your EKG didn’t show changes, but because of your history, we’re concerned about unstable angina."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not another heart attack? But why the pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:21:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It could be reduced blood flow in your heart arteries. Even without a full blockage, the narrowing can cause pain, especially with activity or stress."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:22:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I was climbing stairs earlier in the day and felt short of breath too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That fits. We’re going to start you on a heparin drip to thin your blood and prevent clots. We’re also planning a cardiac catheterization tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:23:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had those deferred before because of my kidney. Is it safe?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:24:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great question. We’ve coordinated with your transplant nephrologist. We’ll use minimal contrast—only 30cc—and give you IV fluids before and after to protect your kidney."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:25:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes me feel better. My creatinine is usually around 1.7."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it was 1.7 on admission. We’ll monitor it closely. We’ve also held your furosemide for now to avoid dehydration."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:26:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my other meds—tacrolimus, sirolimus—are they okay?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:27:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, you’re getting them as usual. Your tacrolimus level this morning was low—less than 2—so the transplant team is watching it, but no change yet."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:28:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had rejection before, so I worry about that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2194-08-21 01:28:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re aware of your history. The team will continue to follow you closely while you’re here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2194-08-21 12:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. You just came back from your cardiac cath. Everything went well. No complications."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2194-08-21 12:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. Did they find anything?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2194-08-21 12:32:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. Your main arteries are okay—no major blockages. But two small branches, the OM1 and OM2, are completely blocked. Because of collateral flow, they didn’t place a stent."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2194-08-21 12:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So nothing can be done?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2194-08-21 12:33:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not with a stent. But we can manage the symptoms with medication. Your cardiologist will see you at your next scheduled visit."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2194-08-21 12:34:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I still get chest pain sometimes. What about that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2194-08-21 12:35:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve started you on a new medication called isosorbide mononitrate—Imdur—to help prevent angina. It works by relaxing your blood vessels."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2194-08-21 12:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will that replace my nitroglycerin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2194-08-21 12:36:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, you’ll still keep the nitroglycerin tablets to use if you get sudden chest pain. Imdur is for prevention."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs are stable. Your creatinine went up a bit to 1.9, but that’s still within your usual range."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that because of the dye from the cath?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly, but we took precautions. It’s only a small rise. We’ve restarted your furosemide this morning since you’re stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:02:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my heart med—metoprolol—was it changed?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we increased it from 50mg to 75mg to better control your heart rate and reduce workload on your heart."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:04:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had low blood pressure before. Should I watch for that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good point. We’ll monitor your pressure. If you feel dizzy or lightheaded, let us know right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2194-08-23 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, we’re planning to discharge you today. You’re doing well—no chest pain, vitals stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2194-08-23 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. What do I need to do after I go home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2194-08-23 09:02:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Continue all your heart meds: aspirin, clopidogrel, atorvastatin, metoprolol, and now the Imdur. Use nitroglycerin if you have chest pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2194-08-23 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my kidney labs?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2194-08-23 09:03:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, get a Chem 7 blood test this week—either Thursday or Friday. The results will be sent directly to your nephrologist."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2194-08-23 09:04:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And follow up with my cardiologist?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2194-08-23 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. He already knows about your cath results and will see you as planned."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2194-08-23 09:05:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel much better now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2194-08-23 09:06:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Call if you have chest pain that doesn’t go away after three nitroglycerin tablets, or if you have trouble breathing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2194-08-23 09:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks again for taking care of me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2194-08-23 09:07:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll get your discharge papers ready, and you should be home soon."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2194-08-21 01:04:00",
    "admission_end": "2194-08-23 14:30:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted with recurrent substernal chest pain radiating to the left shoulder, concerning for unstable angina given her history of multiple NSTEMIs. Initial evaluation showed no troponin elevation or EKG changes, but due to persistent symptoms, she underwent cardiac catheterization which revealed chronic total occlusion of the OM1 and OM2 arteries without intervention possible. She was managed medically with continuation of dual antiplatelet therapy, beta-blockade, and initiation of isosorbide mononitrate for anginal prophylaxis. Her kidney function remained stable with a mild, expected rise in creatinine post-catheterization; nephrology was involved to ensure safe contrast use and medication management. She was discharged home in stable condition with plans for outpatient follow-up in cardiology and nephrology, including repeat renal labs.",
    "problems": [
      "Coronary atherosclerosis of native coronary artery",
      "End stage renal disease",
      "Complications of transplanted kidney",
      "Hypertensive chronic kidney disease, unspecified, with chronic kidney disease stage V or end stage renal disease",
      "Chronic total occlusion of coronary artery",
      "Intermediate coronary syndrome",
      "Old myocardial infarction",
      "Gout, unspecified",
      "Other and unspecified hyperlipidemia",
      "Surgical operation with transplant of whole organ causing abnormal patient reaction, or later complication, without mention of misadventure at time of operation",
      "Personal history of transient ischemic attack (TIA), and cerebral infarction without residual deficits",
      "Personal history of venous thrombosis and embolism",
      "Family history of ischemic heart disease"
    ]
  }
}
